## Why this direction and not the reverse

- **Fails safe.** A route someone adds next week is protected unless they explicitly say otherwise. If instead you decorate each protected controller with `@UseGuards(JwtAuthGuard)`, every new controller starts life **unprotected**, and one forgotten decorator is an open endpoint.
- **Register once.** The guard is a single source of truth rather than copy-pasted across controllers.
- **Ordering, if you add more global guards later:** `APP_GUARD` providers run in the order they're registered/provided, so list auth before any role/permission guard that assumes `request.user` is populated.

## A couple of gotchas worth checking in your repo

- Confirm the token extraction matches whatever your login flow issues (cookie-based JWT would need `request.cookies` instead of the `Authorization` header).
- Keep the exception filter / `UnauthorizedException` shape consistent with the rest of the app so a 401 is rendered the same way everywhere.
- Verify the app still boots with no `UnknownDependenciesException` — a missing `JwtModule` import in `AuthModule` is the usual cause when this guard is added.

