Everything else in the app is protected by default, and any new controller someone adds later fails safe — it's guarded unless it explicitly opts out.

## Why not decorate each protected controller with `@UseGuards(JwtAuthGuard)`?

That's the inversion to avoid: it means "public" is the default and protection is opt-in per controller. The next controller added by anyone who forgets the decorator is silently unprotected. Registering globally with an explicit `@Public()` opt-out flips that — the failure mode is a route that returns 401 and is noticed immediately, not a route that leaks data.

## Two things to watch

- **Don't also stack `@UseGuards(JwtAuthGuard)` on controllers.** With a global `APP_GUARD`, the guard already runs on every route; adding it again just runs it twice. Keep the guard registered in exactly one place.
