If you have several e2e spec files each booting their own app, give every file its own `afterAll(() => app.close())`. Do not rely on closing one and sharing it.

If you want to confirm the diagnosis before changing anything, run Jest with `--detectOpenHandles`. You'll see the open server or timer attributed to the spec that didn't close its app. Re-run after adding `afterAll` and the process should exit on its own.

One caveat: if `createNestApplication()` is paired with `app.listen()`, you must still call `app.close()` — closing the app closes the listening server. Just make sure you don't call `close()` inside the `beforeAll` chain by accident, and don't `process.exit()` your way out of it, since that masks the actual leaked handa rather than fixing them.

